## Summary

Nextflow plugin to render provenance reports for pipeline runs, maintained as a fork by The Jackson Laboratory. Supports standard formats such as [BioCompute Object](https://biocomputeobject.org/) and [Workflow Run RO-Crate](https://www.researchobject.org/workflow-run-crate/).

`nf-prov-jds` is derived from [nf-prov](https://github.com/nextflow-io/nf-prov), developed by the Nextflow team and upstream contributors. The upstream project provides the foundation for this plugin's provenance formats and capture infrastructure. This fork adds per-tool software annotations, an offline metadata explorer with native resource charts, a process-grouped workflow DAG and output lineage, expanded task-script and execution evidence, and reference-only input packaging. It is a separate plugin, not an official release of `nf-prov`.

## Get Started

Build and install from this checkout with `make install` (JDK 21 required). Registry installation requires a published `nf-prov-jds` release.

To enable and configure `nf-prov-jds`, include the following snippet in your Nextflow config and update as needed:

```groovy
plugins {
  id &#x27;nf-prov-jds@0.1.2&#x27;
}

prov {
  enabled = true
  formats {
    bco {
      file = &#x27;bco.json&#x27;
      overwrite = true
    }
    wrroc {
      file = &#x27;ro-crate-metadata.json&#x27;
      overwrite = true
    }
  }
}
```

Any number of formats can be specified. You do not need to modify your pipeline script in order to use the `nf-prov-jds` plugin. When you run your Nextflow pipeline, the plugin will automatically produce the specified provenance reports at the end of the run.

The configuration scope remains `prov`, and software annotations remain `ext.prov`. Replace the `nf-prov` plugin declaration rather than loading both plugins in the same run.

## Examples

After building and installing the plugin, run the bundled example:

```bash
nextflow run nf-prov-jds-test/ -plugins nf-prov-jds@$(cat VERSION)
```

For Workflow Run RO-Crate output, processes can declare the tools they use with `ext.prov.tools`. Each tool is a separate object with optional fields including name (required), version, url, doi, author, description, format, and keywords:

```groovy
process ALIGN {
  ext prov: [
    tools: [
      [
        name: &#x27;STAR&#x27;,
        version: &#x27;2.7.9a&#x27;,
        url: &#x27;https://github.com/alexdobin/STAR&#x27;,
        doi: &#x27;10.1093/bioinformatics/bts635&#x27;,
        author: &#x27;Alexander Dobin&#x27;,
        format: &#x27;application/x-executable&#x27;,
        keywords: [&#x27;alignment&#x27;, &#x27;rnaseq&#x27;],
        description: &#x27;RNA-Seq read alignment.&#x27;
      ]
    ]
  ]

  // input, output and script declarations ...
}
```

See [WRROC configuration and examples](docs/WRROC.md) for software annotations, the HTML explorer, execution evidence and reference-only inputs. Set `prov.formats.wrroc.preview = true` for the offline viewer.

